\subsubsection{QoE Improvement}
As observed in Table \ref{tab:results}, the standard Pensieve agent, when trained with VMAF, converges to a highly conservative policy (Avg VMAF $\approx$ 35), effectively sticking to the lowest bitrate to avoid rebuffering penalties. In contrast, our \textbf{Proposed} method achieves a \textbf{54\% improvement in Average VMAF} compared to Pensieve.

While RobustMPC achieves a higher raw VMAF score, it incurs a significant rebuffering ratio (8.98\%) due to throughput prediction errors in the volatile Norway traces. Our Lyapunov-based reward shaping successfully creates a "safe" exploration zone, keeping rebuffering low (2.74\%) while still aggressively pursuing higher quality when the buffer allows. This balance results in the highest overall QoE (2225.29).

\subsection{Ablation Study}
To verify the contribution of each component, we evaluated variants of our model:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{No-Content:} Removing SI/TI and VMAF lookahead from the state space caused the agent to revert to a behavior similar to Pensieve, failing to distinguish between complex and simple scenes.
    \item \textbf{Fixed-Penalty:} Replacing the dynamic Lyapunov $\beta(B_k)$ with a fixed penalty resulted in increased instability during network drops, proving the necessity of the drift-plus-penalty formulation.
\end{itemize}

% --- Section V: Conclusion ---
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed a novel content-aware ABR framework that bridges the gap between control theory and deep reinforcement learning. By incorporating Spatial and Temporal Information (SI/TI) into the state space, our agent can distinguish between complex scenes requiring high bandwidth and simple scenes where bitrate can be saved. Furthermore, the integration of Lyapunov drift-plus-penalty optimization into the reward function provides a dynamic risk mechanism that ensures buffer stability without manual tuning.

Experimental results on real-world Norway HSDPA traces demonstrated that our approach achieves a 54\% improvement in average VMAF compared to the content-blind Pensieve baseline, while maintaining a low rebuffering ratio comparable to conservative algorithms. Future work will explore the application of this framework to 360-degree video streaming and live streaming scenarios where low latency is critical.

